/**
 * THE MATERIALS THE ROOM IS MADE OF.
 *
 * The frames, rails and screens were flat brown gradients with a generated grain
 * over them — which read as brown, not as anything. These are the supplied oak,
 * leather and linen as colour tiles: each edge folded into its opposite so it
 * repeats with no seam (measured: the seam differs from neighbouring pixels by
 * no more than neighbours differ from each other), the photograph's own detail
 * stretched to a fixed contrast around a fixed dark tone, so type sits on it.
 *
 *   OAK      the frame: header, rails, every dark screen
 *   LEATHER  the bound things: the Codex, the underground shelf
 *   LINEN    bookcloth dyed deep green: tabs and nav buttons
 *   SLATE    honed grey-green slate: the tool screens — bench, inspector, hardware, press
 *   PLASTER  limewash, shaded down to take light type: the crew and the notices
 *   FELT     dark pine-green wool: Orders and contracts, the one green surface
 *   STRAW    a rice-straw mat: the koji room's own strips
 *   HESSIAN  sacking: behind the pantry list in the rail
 *   CEDAR    pale cedar boards, shaded: the koji room's frame, the room it opens onto
 *   WALNUT   dark walnut: the archive entries in the Codex, where the records are kept
 *   GREYOAK  weathered grey oak: the cellar, which is older and colder than the workshop
 *
 * Dark enough that --text-hi and --text-mid pass AA against the LIGHT end of
 * each tile (95th percentile), which is where type is hardest to read.
 * Printed stock (labels, cards, dockets) is not on this list and keeps the linen
 * weave in paperArt.ts. Data URIs, because the published page's CSP blocks images.
 */
export const OAK_TILE = 'data:image/webp;base64,';
export const LEATHER_TILE = 'data:image/webp;base64,';
export const LINEN_TILE = 'data:image/webp;base64,';
